WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.134
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.134 --> 00:00:16.091
[CHEER]

00:00:16.091 --> 00:00:18.321
&gt;&gt; This primal, guttural chant

00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:24.110
explodes to the throats of 250 boys and
girls signaling the start of the camp day.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.350
But I have no idea what's going on.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.430
I've arrived at camp a week late and
I feel lost.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.400
Can you imagine that scared seven
year old trembling in the back row?

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:38.580
That's me.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.560
Terrified, I won't belong.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.880
Scared, I won't make friends.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.180
That was 20 years ago, and

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.970
I was about to learn the lesson I
want to share with you all now.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.928
The profound impact an invested
adult can have on a child's life.

00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:01.300
For me, that adult was Tarzan Team.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.580
Back in the summer of 2002 Tarzan Team
was the coolest guy around.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.660
He wore Ray Bans and
a visor before the retro look was in.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.830
He was bigger, faster and
stronger than everyone I can't.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.750
In Tarzan Team set out to be my friend.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.495
Me, that skinny seven-year-old,

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:26.384
had more anxiety in his gut
than he could handle himself.

00:01:26.384 --> 00:01:32.366
I remember the way that Tarzan Team
made me feel, feel like I belonged.

00:01:32.366 --> 00:01:36.890
He would say things like, whoa,
Mario sure knows how to make a mud ball.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:43.770
You all should be asking him how he does
it Mario that's a fantastic dream catcher.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.486
We'll have to hang it in our secret for
these small moments of investment

00:01:48.486 --> 00:01:53.609
turned a terrifying experience into
a summer of social and emotional growth.

00:01:55.485 --> 00:01:58.790
Tarzan Team invested in me,
and it transformed my life.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:06.362
Summer Camp has the ability to inspire
transformative change on people's lives,

00:02:06.362 --> 00:02:06.970
full stop.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.544
The National Academy of Sciences
highlights summer camp benefits on child

00:02:12.544 --> 00:02:16.790
safety, social, emotional well-being,
physical and mental health, and

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:17.970
academic learning.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.789
An ongoing longitudinal study by
psychologist David Yeager indicates

00:02:23.789 --> 00:02:28.516
that a mere one week at camp can increase
college attendance rates by 18%.

00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:31.670
And that's one week.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.320
Imagine the power of a full summer.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.215
So the stats tell us we do good by our
children when we send them to camp with

00:02:39.215 --> 00:02:41.599
a power of an invested
adult works wonders.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.120
But that's not the only thing I'm
here to talk about with you today.

00:02:48.176 --> 00:02:55.636
This is picture of me and Tarzan Team,
back in that fabled summer of 2002.

00:02:55.636 --> 00:02:59.360
Beyond the smiles in
the mud stained shirts,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.458
what stands out to me,
the general lack of ethnic diversity.

00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:08.750
My memory may be fuzzy, but

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:13.556
I'm willing to bet that Team was
the only this summer at camp.

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:20.250
I can say across my 16 years at camp, Team
was the only black counselor in my group.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:27.140
The American Camp Association states that
70% of summer campers are white Americans.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.314
And this under represents the true figure
at private for profit sleep-away camps.

00:03:32.314 --> 00:03:34.634
Across the entire camping landscape,

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:38.510
only 5% of camps cater to
a majority impoverished population.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:45.342
It pains me to think about what it was
like for Tarzan Team to run around tying

00:03:45.342 --> 00:03:50.070
shoes and applying band aids in a place
where no one looks like he did.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.964
I won't presume to speak for him.

00:03:54.964 --> 00:03:58.368
But I do know what it's
like to be the only.

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:05.661
Across three years majoring consulting
in 12 different projects, not once did

00:04:05.661 --> 00:04:10.900
I have a black or Latinx supervisor,
team manager, or managing partner.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.181
When I looked upwards,
craving a mentor that looked like me,

00:04:16.181 --> 00:04:19.700
I struggled to find a business
leader I can connect with.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.390
And now I'm here at the GSB and
I'm struck by this duality.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:30.040
On one hand we learned so
much about the benefits of diversity.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.751
Research right here at Stanford
tells us that diverse groups are 87%

00:04:34.751 --> 00:04:36.721
better at making decisions.

00:04:36.721 --> 00:04:41.581
A recent study by the Boston Consulting
Group says that diverse management

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:45.218
teams lead their companies
to 19% higher revenues.

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:49.119
Better at making money, better at
making decision and yet I still see so

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:50.690
much room for improvement.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.123
Black and Latin X individuals attend
college 10 to 20% less than their

00:04:56.123 --> 00:04:56.860
white peers.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.820
Even with a degree,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.780
Black college graduates earn 21%
less than their White classmates.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.900
And this is exacerbated at the top.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.865
Were racially diverse executives,
executives of any non-white American

00:05:12.865 --> 00:05:16.595
background hold only 16%
of total C suite positions.

00:05:19.044 --> 00:05:20.410
I want to see this change.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.941
Creating and upholding diverse spaces
matters to me, because I dream of

00:05:26.941 --> 00:05:31.620
seeing people in color in positions
of power, feeling they belong.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.760
Expanding access to summer camp
can be a big part of this change.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.640
I know because for me,
Camp made all the difference.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.362
Camp allowed me to beat back that
Gremlin in my head screeching,

00:05:47.362 --> 00:05:50.610
you're an imposter, you don't belong.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.450
I remember back in middle school
when I was dealing with bullying,

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.564
Camp was the haven in which I could
prove to myself that I could be liked,

00:05:58.564 --> 00:06:01.530
I could make friends I
could respond to adversity.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:09.820
Simply Put Camp and its leaders like
Tarzy Team showed me I belonged.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:13.964
And if I could go from outsider to
belonging at camp I sure as hell could do

00:06:13.964 --> 00:06:15.219
it in the real world.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:21.212
So we know we do well by our children
when we send them to Camp and

00:06:21.212 --> 00:06:26.155
we know we do well by society when
we promote in uphold diverse spaces.

00:06:26.155 --> 00:06:30.047
How do we bring these two truths together?

00:06:30.047 --> 00:06:33.979
I could deliver a whole other talk
about what society can do to diversify

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:35.405
the Camp space.

00:06:35.405 --> 00:06:39.276
From directing tax dollars to camp
initiatives, to increasing education for

00:06:39.276 --> 00:06:41.572
minority parents about
the benefits of Camp.

00:06:42.822 --> 00:06:43.459
and even yet

00:06:43.459 --> 00:06:47.867
I can deliver this whole other talk about
the Camp industry could do to diversify.

00:06:47.867 --> 00:06:51.843
From targeting more diverse camp directors
to increasing diversity, equity,

00:06:51.843 --> 00:06:53.602
inclusion training for its staff.

00:06:55.222 --> 00:06:56.502
But I want to focus on the here and now.

00:06:57.902 --> 00:07:01.842
I want to focus on what you in this
room can do to expand access to

00:07:01.842 --> 00:07:05.650
these crucially important
youth development programs.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.010
First, send your kids to Camp and
do so thoughtfully.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.808
You may not have kids now, but if in when
you do send them out there to play in

00:07:16.808 --> 00:07:20.339
the woods, make mud balls and
stampede through the wilderness.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:24.060
If that's not their thing, that's okay.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.669
Send them to Basketball Camp, Theater
Camp, Science Camp, Woodworking Camp,

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:30.460
imaginary skateboarding with
a parrot on your shoulder camp.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.240
The options are really endless.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.120
But choose that camp deliberately.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.279
Select a camp that upholds
a diverse environment.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.313
Vote with your wallet and
demonstrate to camp owners and

00:07:44.313 --> 00:07:46.230
directors across the nation.

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:49.019
That providing an enriching experience for

00:07:49.019 --> 00:07:52.570
children of all backgrounds
is a business imperative.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.850
And secondly,
donate to scholarship programs.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:02.998
Just about every camp organization has
its own campership program focused on

00:08:02.998 --> 00:08:04.926
expanding opportunity of access.

00:08:04.926 --> 00:08:08.904
The truth is, however, many of these
slots go on applied for because minority

00:08:08.904 --> 00:08:12.372
families either incorrectly
believe they can't afford camp or

00:08:12.372 --> 00:08:14.570
are afraid to send their children away.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:18.941
Certain nonprofit organizations
are addressing this issue by

00:08:18.941 --> 00:08:22.839
directly matching inner city families
with residential summer camp programs.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.551
Via donations from individuals like
you and me, organizations such as

00:08:28.551 --> 00:08:33.232
the Guardsmen, Camp Piles, and
Project Mori, pay full camp tuition, so

00:08:33.232 --> 00:08:38.610
that financial burdens don't bar children
from these life-changing programmes.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:41.770
I'll confess,

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:46.630
diversifying the camp space won't fix all
the achievement gaps I mentioned earlier.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:53.835
Even so, I can assert with
conviction that a summer at camp

00:08:53.835 --> 00:08:59.053
can fundamentally shift
the life trajectory of a child,

00:09:01.598 --> 00:09:09.014
It can show them they belong,
it did for me.

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:13.489
I started this talk by telling
you about Tarzan Team in

00:09:13.489 --> 00:09:16.181
the power of an invested adult.

00:09:16.181 --> 00:09:21.603
Now I'm asking you to
be that invested adult.

00:09:21.603 --> 00:09:22.672
Thank you.

00:09:22.672 --> 00:09:24.145
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:24.145 --> 00:09:28.934
[MUSIC]

